service, please leave a comment.

00:00:23.519 --> 00:00:24.330
Hi Anna.

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:26.339
Hello, Steve.

00:00:26.339 --> 00:00:27.299
Nice to meet you.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.379
Nice to meet you.

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:33.600
So, uh, I often talk about learning
other languages, you know, right now

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.520
it's Persian and Arabic or people are
learning Japanese or French but you

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:42.575
teach English, which is the ...  probably
the language, by far in fact, the

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:45.035
language that most people are learning.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:49.445
So I'm a native speaker of English, but
I'm curious, first of all, I have a number

00:00:49.445 --> 00:00:50.975
of questions, question number one...

00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:56.790
oh, by the way, we leave a link to
english Like a Native in our description

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.890
box so people can go there and find you.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:03.450
And I know a lot of people listen to my
videos in order to improve their English.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.420
And so maybe they should, uh,
they would benefit in fact, by

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.339
going to your YouTube channel.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:15.450
Now, first question where, where do most
of, what are the main sort of countries

00:01:15.539 --> 00:01:19.260
of, uh, you know, the nationalities of
people who are learning English with you?

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.365
Uh, so with me, I mean, as you can
imagine, I've got people from all over

00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:31.290
the world, but I'd say the majority
of my learners, funnily enough, is

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:39.300
UK, US, uh, Italy, India and, and
then it really kind of dissipates.

00:01:39.305 --> 00:01:39.390
Right.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.530
So you've got Brazil, but that's
because, um, a lot of the people who

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:49.360
watch my content are actually living and
working in English speaking countries

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.860
and so they really have that need and
motivation to improve their English.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.479
And so that's why I have this kind of
demographic in English speaking countries.

00:01:58.839 --> 00:01:58.960
Right.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:03.789
But, um, outside of the UK and
us, it is, uh, Brazil, India,

00:02:03.999 --> 00:02:05.890
uh, and Europe across Europe.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:06.369
Right.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:07.830
Um, Yeah.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.850
Now, so then that's why I asked
sort of the nationality or the, the

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.890
ethnicity of, of these learners.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.180
So what would be the main sort of
ethnic groups within the UK or the US?

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:20.220
Ah...

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.530
what are the native languages?

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:22.200
Yeah.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:31.440
It's Italian, British, um, Italian
Brazilian, um, Indian um,  Polish,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.725
Russian, it's it's a real mix.

00:02:34.785 --> 00:02:35.125
A real mix.

00:02:35.125 --> 00:02:35.505
It is a real mix.

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:36.045
Okay.

00:02:36.105 --> 00:02:36.675
Yeah.

00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:38.805
You're talking about small percentages.

00:02:38.835 --> 00:02:39.585
Right.

00:02:39.585 --> 00:02:40.275
Across the globe.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.395
And, and what, what are the
major difficulties that people

00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:45.075
experience in learning English?

00:02:45.075 --> 00:02:51.840
Uh, So, um, across the board, many of
them have confidence issues and that

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.780
really starts to present itself when
it comes to speaking the language.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:00.060
So many of them get to themselves
to an upper intermediate, even at

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:06.240
lower advanced level that still
feel unable to communicate vocally

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.320
and they kind of hit a wall.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.680
They can't recall the vocabulary.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.990
They they're thinking too much about the
grammar and the structure of the sentences

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:21.490
and they just feel really inhibited, um,
not wanting to make mistakes, uh, and

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:23.410
worried about pronunciation and things.

00:03:23.415 --> 00:03:27.640
So I think one of the things we try
and do on English Like a Native is

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.770
just to kind of break down that fear.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:36.600
Um, and also just to encourage them to
speak as much as possible, you know?

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.620
Um, you know, it's really
important to just get going right.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.480
To make mistakes.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.040
I mean, that's not unique
to people learning English.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.470
That's common to people
learning any language.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.090
Would you say that to some extent,
their fear of making mistakes almost

00:03:51.095 --> 00:03:53.250
makes them make more mistakes?

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.920
I, well, I think it's true
in anything you're learning.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.460
If you, if you get scared, if
your brain is scared and you get

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.980
those stress hormones, your brain
doesn't function in the same way

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.180
as it would when you're relaxed.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.820
And so yeah I would say
you do make more mistakes.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:09.870
Oh yeah.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.485
And you become it's like, I, I
always say to my students to imagine

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:16.930
there's a bus going down the hill
and the bus has no brakes and it's

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.880
getting faster and faster and faster.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:23.740
And you can either choose to sit at the
back of the bus and cower and let the

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:28.690
nerves just take over and ruin everything
or you can jump into the driver's seat

00:04:28.695 --> 00:04:33.940
and at least steer those nerves, just
and, you know, take control of that energy

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:38.650
and, and change the energy into a positive
energy is what I always try and push.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.280
Well, I mean, it is true if we're, if
we're at a party somewhere and somebody

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.095
walks in and you know, who is that person?

00:04:46.095 --> 00:04:48.915
And you try to force yourself
to remember the name.

00:04:49.095 --> 00:04:51.645
You won't remember the name, the
more pressure you put on yourself

00:04:51.645 --> 00:04:55.155
to try to remember something the
less likely you are to remember it.

00:04:55.155 --> 00:04:56.655
So, uh, yeah.

00:04:56.955 --> 00:05:00.735
So is, is the concern though,
is it more that they feel they,

00:05:00.765 --> 00:05:02.835
they have problems with grammar?

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:05.955
Is it that they don't have
enough vocabulary or is it

00:05:05.955 --> 00:05:07.665
concerns about pronunciation?

00:05:08.624 --> 00:05:12.885
So with English pronunciation
is tricky because our spellings

00:05:12.885 --> 00:05:15.254
do not inform the pronunciation.

00:05:15.254 --> 00:05:15.344
Right?

00:05:15.344 --> 00:05:16.604
Our spellings are crazy.

00:05:16.604 --> 00:05:19.694
And I think American English is easier.

00:05:20.025 --> 00:05:26.984
Your system for writing, um, is, is,
is much, it makes more sense, right?

00:05:26.984 --> 00:05:30.344
For the pronunciation, but the
British English spellings do

00:05:30.344 --> 00:05:32.234
not make as much sense at all.

00:05:32.294 --> 00:05:33.734
Well, I hadn't even focused on that.

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:34.695
How are they different?

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.160
So for example, um, example
is, uh, flavor or color.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:40.900
So...

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:41.070
oh yeah.

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:45.630
so colour American English
is C O L O R, but in British

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:46.830
English, we have a U in there.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.150
So it's C O L O U R.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.440
But we have that in Canada, too.

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:51.270
We have that in Canada, too.

00:05:51.299 --> 00:05:51.330
Okay.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.020
But the...

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:56.520
never really thought of it as a problem,
but the bigger issues are like, uh,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:02.909
although and bough and through and cough
and rough are all written with O U G

00:06:02.914 --> 00:06:04.440
H and they're pronounced differently.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.249
And that's true on both
sides of the Atlantic.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.780
So yeah, English spelling is a problem.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:13.439
And you think then the pronunciation,
the difficulties people have in

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:15.840
English with English, pronunciation
comes from the spelling, or

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.189
that's just one of the factors.

00:06:17.379 --> 00:06:18.950
It's, it's one of the factors.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:20.640
Um and that there aren't...

00:06:20.849 --> 00:06:25.719
the rules aren't very consistent
either with pronunciation so that's

00:06:25.739 --> 00:06:29.549
tricky, but also one of the biggest
issues for learning English is the

00:06:29.549 --> 00:06:34.364
idiomatic language and the phrasal
verbs because there's no blanket rule.

00:06:34.364 --> 00:06:36.705
Again, you have to learn
on a case by case basis.

00:06:36.705 --> 00:06:42.614
And we use so many phrase of herbs and so
many idioms in just day to day speaking

00:06:42.619 --> 00:06:48.015
that you can know the vocabulary, but not
know the phrases and so that's tricky and

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.564
that just like just requires exposure.

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:55.080
You just have to keep exposing
yourself to that kind of

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.750
language, um, to native speakers.

00:06:58.049 --> 00:07:03.330
And, and this is so the name of my, my
channel English Like a Native is  it's

00:07:03.335 --> 00:07:09.840
a little bit of, um, people see it as a
negative thing sometimes in the industry

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.080
to, to do something like a native, you'll
never be a native of that language.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.669
So why should you try to be like a native.

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:15.030
Right.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.944
That was, that was never
the point of my brand name.

00:07:17.944 --> 00:07:25.299
The brand name is more about acquiring
and learning like a native does, so

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:30.989
not stressing about grammar rules and
spending more time just listening and

00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:34.289
enjoying language in a, in the same
way you would your target language.

00:07:34.289 --> 00:07:40.259
So watching films in, in your target
language and, um, hanging out with

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:43.590
friends and speaking in your target
language, in a relaxed environment,

00:07:43.619 --> 00:07:48.090
and just trying to enjoy it rather
than sit and study and study the rules.

00:07:48.945 --> 00:07:52.815
To be honest, most natives do
not know their own language in

00:07:52.815 --> 00:07:54.195
the way that a non-native would.

00:07:54.195 --> 00:07:55.755
We don't know the grammar rules.

00:07:56.145 --> 00:07:57.705
We, we never study them.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:58.724
We just acquire them.

00:07:58.729 --> 00:08:02.025
We just understand that something
sounds right or something sounds wrong.

00:08:02.025 --> 00:08:04.544
And sometimes we make mistakes.

00:08:05.085 --> 00:08:05.684
As a set...

00:08:05.684 --> 00:08:10.304
as a standard thing across the UK,
you know, in the north, in the north,

00:08:10.304 --> 00:08:15.124
there'll be, um, grammar, mistakes that
are just an accepted way of speaking.

00:08:15.154 --> 00:08:16.455
It's just what we say.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:17.775
Well, we have that everywhere now.

00:08:17.775 --> 00:08:21.825
I mean, in North America, you hear people
say I would've went, I would've went.

00:08:22.364 --> 00:08:22.694
Right.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:22.884
Okay.

00:08:22.884 --> 00:08:25.094
You know, that's, it's
becoming more and more common.

00:08:25.094 --> 00:08:28.034
It's just, it sounds so bad to
me, but a lot of people say that.

00:08:28.515 --> 00:08:31.469
So um, yeah, I agree with you.

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:35.459
You know, I think the native is the
model, you can't get away from that.

00:08:35.459 --> 00:08:36.509
The native is the model.

00:08:36.899 --> 00:08:41.219
Uh, even though we're not gonna sound
like a native, but that's in fact we wanna

00:08:41.219 --> 00:08:42.959
get as close as possible to the native.

00:08:43.319 --> 00:08:46.469
It's a bit of a problem in English
because even within a country like

00:08:46.469 --> 00:08:51.329
the UK, there's so many regional
accents, so which native are you going?

00:08:52.125 --> 00:08:56.714
You know, which, which form of the
language, which type of pronunciation is,

00:08:56.714 --> 00:08:58.755
is the most popular with your learners?

00:08:59.324 --> 00:09:00.525
Who do they wanna sound like?

00:09:00.944 --> 00:09:05.474
Well, I, I, on my channel,
I cover a number of regional

00:09:05.474 --> 00:09:07.665
accents, regional British accents.

00:09:07.724 --> 00:09:11.055
And, um, I do also cover a lot
of differences between American

00:09:11.055 --> 00:09:13.795
English and British English
because people are interested.

00:09:14.614 --> 00:09:18.044
American English is more globally studied.

00:09:18.344 --> 00:09:18.704
Um...

00:09:18.914 --> 00:09:19.094
oh really?

00:09:19.185 --> 00:09:19.574
Okay.

00:09:19.574 --> 00:09:19.994
Yeah.

00:09:19.994 --> 00:09:21.554
And in, and it's the same in America.

00:09:21.554 --> 00:09:24.854
Of course, there's lots of
variations of American accents

00:09:24.854 --> 00:09:26.444
that are wildly different.

00:09:26.894 --> 00:09:34.114
Um, but what I always, I teach RP, which
in fact, there aren't, there are not many

00:09:34.114 --> 00:09:37.755
people in the UK who actually use RP...

00:09:38.915 --> 00:09:42.604
Now, just to explain to
my audience, what is  RP?

00:09:42.609 --> 00:09:42.635
RP.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:48.214
RP is received pronunciation and it's the
standard, people don't like this word,

00:09:48.305 --> 00:09:52.805
natives don't like this word, but it's the
standard accent of, of British, because

00:09:53.224 --> 00:09:55.245
it's, what's written in the dictionaries.

00:09:55.545 --> 00:09:56.654
It's the, the...

00:09:56.795 --> 00:09:58.295
it's non regional.

00:09:58.334 --> 00:09:59.714
It doesn't belong to a region.

00:09:59.714 --> 00:10:04.935
It was introduced many, many years
ago as a way of speaking, if you were

00:10:04.964 --> 00:10:09.584
educated and this is why it has a bad,
a bad reputation in the UK, because if

00:10:09.584 --> 00:10:16.259
you speak like this with a, an RP, then
people assume that you are very wealthy

00:10:16.469 --> 00:10:19.649
and, and snobby, posh, well educated.

00:10:19.889 --> 00:10:22.209
And that you look down
on the lower classes.

00:10:22.459 --> 00:10:26.249
Which is crazy now, because
that's not, is, it is what it is.

00:10:26.249 --> 00:10:27.869
I don't wanna open that can of worms.

00:10:28.889 --> 00:10:28.979
Right.

00:10:28.979 --> 00:10:28.980
But.

00:10:29.355 --> 00:10:30.405
What's important.

00:10:30.435 --> 00:10:34.005
I think if you are learning, if
you want to learn British English

00:10:34.005 --> 00:10:37.064
or any variation of English, I
don't think it really matters.

00:10:37.125 --> 00:10:40.964
It has to be something you want to learn,
like what you feel passionate about,

00:10:40.964 --> 00:10:46.185
because I can understand anyone speaking
English, what, whatever variation it

00:10:46.185 --> 00:10:51.105
is, there might be few little regional
differences, dialect that's different.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:55.920
But generally it doesn't matter
which version you go for, but if

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.219
you are going to move to an English
speaking country, I'd always

00:10:59.219 --> 00:11:01.020
say, perhaps it's interesting...

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:06.060
it would be good for you to learn or
become familiar with that particular

00:11:06.319 --> 00:11:10.654
regional accent or at least the
dialect, because for example,

00:11:11.064 --> 00:11:14.764
scousers in Liverpool, in the north
of England have a very different

00:11:14.764 --> 00:11:17.344
way of talking than, than I do.

00:11:17.464 --> 00:11:22.295
And if you learn from me and never exposed
yourself to a scouse accent, or any of

00:11:22.295 --> 00:11:25.535
their dialect, you'd arrive there and
not have a clue what anyone was saying.

00:11:26.165 --> 00:11:26.344
Right.

00:11:26.374 --> 00:11:31.515
So I think, um, it depends on
your situation and also what

00:11:31.535 --> 00:11:32.734
you are passionate about.

00:11:33.425 --> 00:11:33.724
Yeah.

00:11:33.964 --> 00:11:34.685
I mean, I agree.

00:11:34.685 --> 00:11:38.265
And I think that it's true to a
large extent with any language.

00:11:38.295 --> 00:11:42.645
Uh, when I was learning Portuguese
or Spanish, uh, I would listen to

00:11:42.675 --> 00:11:46.064
Portuguese from Europe, Portuguese
from Brazil, because my goal is

00:11:46.069 --> 00:11:47.925
comprehension but then if I'm...

00:11:47.984 --> 00:11:51.464
I find that when we are learning another
language, we're very easily influenced.

00:11:51.464 --> 00:11:54.764
Like our own, our accent in our
own language is not gonna change.

00:11:54.764 --> 00:11:58.064
I can live in the UK for 20 years,
I'll still speak the way I speak now.

00:11:58.394 --> 00:12:02.634
But if I'm in France, if I'm in
Southern France, if I'm in Quebec, if

00:12:02.654 --> 00:12:06.374
I'm in Brazil, I find that the, these
languages that I have learned I'm

00:12:06.374 --> 00:12:10.334
easily influenced by the people around
me so that okay, my accent will kind

00:12:10.334 --> 00:12:14.639
of, you know, adapt to the environment,
but, but the key is, is pronunciation.

00:12:14.639 --> 00:12:14.969
I agree.

00:12:14.999 --> 00:12:16.379
Excuse me, is comprehension.

00:12:16.439 --> 00:12:18.089
Uh, we've gotta be able to understand.

00:12:18.089 --> 00:12:20.309
So I think people should listen
to all kinds of different accents.

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:23.129
Getting back to this phrasal
verbs that always comes up.

00:12:23.129 --> 00:12:27.839
So do you have a special technique for
people or you just say, you know, just

00:12:27.839 --> 00:12:30.719
expose yourself to a lot of the language
and you'll eventually get used to them?

00:12:31.049 --> 00:12:31.349
Yeah.

00:12:31.349 --> 00:12:31.829
Yeah.

00:12:31.829 --> 00:12:38.129
So I have, I actually have a, a challenge
that I run, um, a couple of times a year.

00:12:38.129 --> 00:12:42.029
And it's a 30 day challenge where
I'll teach 300 phrasal verbs in 30

00:12:42.029 --> 00:12:45.269
days, and the method is quite simple.

00:12:45.269 --> 00:12:48.314
It's a 30 days of immersing yourself.

00:12:48.314 --> 00:12:51.404
So you have to give at
least 20 to 30 minutes.

00:12:51.435 --> 00:12:55.244
Most of my students who do it, do
about an hour a day, and it doesn't

00:12:55.244 --> 00:12:57.104
matter if you miss a day, you, it is...

00:12:57.104 --> 00:12:58.144
all the material's there.

00:12:58.144 --> 00:13:05.504
You can catch up but it's, it, it, the
method that I use is to just use the

00:13:05.504 --> 00:13:08.535
vocabulary at least four or five times.

00:13:08.535 --> 00:13:11.264
So every phrasal verb you'll
be introduced to in different

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.364
ways, you'll have to write it.

